{#
    The demo's own strip of chrome: what this is, and the button that makes
    mail arrive.

    Fixed to the bottom rather than pushed in above the app. plMail's layout is
    a full-height three-pane grid, and a banner in the flow shortens the reading
    pane on every page — so the first thing a visitor would learn about the
    product is that it does not quite fit. It also has to survive Turbo
    navigation, which it does by living in the layout rather than in a page.

    Left of the button is the disclosure, and it is deliberately the larger
    half. Somebody who has been handed a working mailbox on a public URL is
    owed a plain statement that it is not theirs and will be deleted.
#}
<div class="fixed bottom-0 inset-x-0 z-50 border-t border-line bg-surface/95 backdrop-blur
            px-4 py-2 flex items-center gap-4 text-sm"
     id="demo-bar">

    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 min-w-0">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-flask text-accent" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <span class="font-medium text-ink">{{ 'demo.bar.title'|trans }}</span>
        <span class="text-ink-muted truncate hidden sm:inline">{{ 'demo.bar.blurb'|trans }}</span>
    </div>

    {#
        Turbo drives this deliberately. With data-turbo="false" the POST was a
        full browser navigation, so pressing Receive reloaded the whole page —
        on top of mail that had ALREADY appeared over Mercure a moment earlier.
        The visitor saw the message arrive, then the list blink and replay its
        entry animation, which reads as the app doing the work twice.

        Now the response is a stream that touches only this bar. The mail itself
        arrives the way real mail does: pushed, in place, with nothing else
        moving. That is the whole thing the button exists to demonstrate, and
        the reload was hiding it.
    #}
    <form method="post"
          action="{{ path('app_demo_receive') }}"
          class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-3 shrink-0">
        <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('demo_receive') }}">

        {# Naming the next delivery is what keeps the button from being a
           mystery box: a visitor who has pressed it twice can see that a third
           press brings something else. #}
        {% if demo.nextSubject is not null %}
            <span class="text-ink-muted hidden md:inline">
                {{ 'demo.bar.next'|trans({'%subject%': demo.nextSubject}) }}
            </span>
        {% endif %}

        <button type="submit"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg
                       bg-accent text-on-accent font-medium hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-inbox" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            {{ 'demo.bar.receive'|trans }}
        </button>
    </form>
</div>
